          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-jane-eyre-5da4cfc02f8bc2c8\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Jane directly avows that she loves St. John in a specifically sisterly sense. The utterance presents her affectionate state as true while delimiting it from romantic love and marital commitment. It neither undertakes a new obligation nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The phrase “as a sister” positions their relationship, but in this argumentative context it qualifies the kind of love asserted rather than enacting a substantial relational undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Jane directly addresses St. John during their dispute over marriage and accompanying him to India.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Jane uses the avowal to explain why she would have risked much by accompanying St. John while firmly rejecting his claim that she is obligated to go, his marriage proposal, and any romantic interpretation of her affection. The sisterly qualification is contrastive and boundary-setting: she cares for and trusts him, but her heart remains attached to Rochester. The controlled explanatory syntax supplies no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive force.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“because I admire, confide in, and, as a sister, I love you”\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional support: Jane explicitly presents sisterly love, alongside admiration and trust, as her emotional attitude toward St. John.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case. I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within Jane’s explicit denial of obligation, weighing against performative or commitment-making force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Jane has just said, “I will be your curate, if you like, but never your wife.”\",\"supports\":\"The sisterly qualification distinguishes affection from romantic or marital commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The passage later confirms that Jane’s heart turns toward Mr. Rochester, which she acknowledges by silence.\",\"supports\":\"Supports reading the utterance as a sincere but carefully delimited avowal of non-romantic love for St. John.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The sisterly boundary and argumentative purpose are contextual qualifications rather than missing dimensions of core force; P, E, and O are unnecessary.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
